---
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { routeUrl } from "../lib/url.ts";

/*
 * GitHub Pages and Cloudflare Pages both serve /404.html for an unmatched
 * path, so this needs no route configuration, only to exist. The legacy site
 * had one; the rebuild did not, which meant every mistyped URL fell through to
 * the host's default page with none of this site's chrome on it.
 *
 * The legacy page was a Shakespearean pastiche. That particular joke does not
 * survive the rebrand, but its premise does: a 404 is read more attentively
 * than almost any other page here, because the reader has stopped and is
 * looking at the address bar for the first time all day. It is worth writing.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Not found"
  description="A well-formed address that refers to nothing, and a short note on why that is more interesting than it sounds."
>
  <PageIntro
    kind="page"
    eyebrow="404"
    title="This address is perfectly well formed, and refers to nothing."
    lede="Which is a stranger state of affairs than the status code makes it sound."
  >
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-xs">
      <Button href={routeUrl("/")} tone="primary" size="control">Home</Button>
      <Button href={routeUrl("/blog")} tone="secondary" size="control">Blog</Button>
      <Button href={routeUrl("/docs")} tone="secondary" size="control">Docs</Button>
    </div>
  </PageIntro>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Russell ran into this in 1905, without the benefit of a browser.
      &ldquo;The present King of France&rdquo; is grammatical, unambiguous, and
      built entirely out of words that mean things. It still picks out nobody.
      A name can be in perfect working order and have nothing on the other end
      of it, and a sentence containing one is not thereby nonsense. It is
      simply false.
    </p>
    <p>
      The address you just followed is in that condition. The server parsed it,
      understood it, went looking, and came back with the web's version of
      &ldquo;there is no such thing&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <p>
      What it did not tell you is whether there ever was. A 404 is deliberately
      silent on the point. There is a separate code, 410 Gone, for the stronger
      claim that a page existed and has been deliberately withdrawn, and it is
      vanishingly rare in the wild precisely because almost nobody keeps records
      good enough to assert it. Not finding something gets a status code of its
      own; knowing it is absent turns out to be much more expensive.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the ordinary case one of us has made a typing mistake, and it is not
      usually you. If a link on this site sent you here, that is my bug, and the
      contact section of the <a href={routeUrl("/about")}>about page</a> is the
      fastest way to tell me about it.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
